### Step 4: Customize CSS and Layouts

Jekyll allows you to override any file inside a theme without touching the gem files. The precedence order is simple: **any file in your project folder overrides the theme's default file of the same name and path.**

#### 1. Add Custom CSS

Create a custom stylesheet file at `assets/css/style.scss`:

```scss
---
---
@import "{{ site.theme }}";

/* Add custom overrides below */
body {
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
}

a {
  color: #0066cc;
}

```

*(The two lines of triple dashes `---` at the top are mandatory—they tell Jekyll to compile the SCSS file).*

#### 2. Override Layouts or Partials

If you want to modify the header, footer, or HTML structure:

1. Create a folder named `_includes` or `_layouts` in your project root.
2. Add files such as `_includes/header.html` or `_includes/footer.html` to replace the theme's built-in header or footer with your own HTML.